colorcorrect
by Shunsuke Aihara - http://argmax.jp

date::

    February 2012

description::

    Imprement some of color correction algorithms

require::

    python3.10 or later
    numpy>=2.0
    Pillow>=10.0
    a C++ compiler (the block based algorithms and the sampled ACE are in C++)

install::

    % pip install .

    (a C++ compiler is needed; "python -m build" creates sdist and wheel)

test::

    % pip install -e .
    % python -m unittest discover -s test -p "*_test.py"

impement algorithms::

    gray world
         colorcorrect.algorithm.gray_world
         usage: image
    max white
        colorcorrect.algorithm.max_white
        usage: image
    stretch
        colorcorrect.algorithm.stretch
        usage: image
    retinex
        colorcorrect.algorithm.retinex
        usage: image
    retinex with adjust (gray world + retinex quadratic mapping)
            colorcorrect.algorithm.retinex_with_adjust
            usage: image
    standard deviation weighted grey world
             colorcorrect.algorithm.standard_deviation_weighted_grey_world
             usage: image,subblock width(default:20), subblock height(default:20)
    standard deviation and luminance weighted gray world
             colorcorrect.algorithm.standard_deviation_and_luminance_weighted_gray_world
             usage: image,subblock width(default:20), subblock height(default:20)
    luminance weighted gray world
              colorcorrect.algorithm.luminance_weighted_gray_world
             usage: image,subblock width(default:20), subblock height(default:20)
    (each block based algorithm also has a pure numpy twin with a _python suffix)
    automatic color equalization
              colorcorrect.algorithm.automatic_color_equalization
              usage: image,slope(default:10),limit(default:1000),samples(default:500),
                     method(default:"fft"),levels(default:16),seed(default:None),workers(default:None),
                     min_distance(default:None),preserve_chroma(default:False)
              method="fft" evaluates the full image sum of the paper with FFT
              convolutions (exact up to the interpolation over `levels` grey
              levels, deterministic, multi-threaded).  method="sampling" is the
              Monte-Carlo C++ implementation comparing every pixel with
              `samples` random pixels; pass `seed` for reproducible output and
              `min_distance` (default: half the mean sample spacing) to keep a
              sample right next to a pixel from dominating it.
              The saturation function is r(x) = clip(slope*x, -limit, limit) on
              pixel differences in [-255, 255]; only slope/limit matters.
              preserve_chroma=True runs ACE on the luminance only and keeps the
              colour of every pixel (an extension of the paper); combined with
              retinex_with_adjust it adds local contrast without desaturating:

              >>> cca.automatic_color_equalization(cca.retinex_with_adjust(img), preserve_chroma=True)

    shades of gray
              colorcorrect.algorithm.shades_of_gray
              usage: image,p(default:6),saturation_threshold(default:255)
              Minkowski p-norm grey world (p=1 grey world, p=None max-RGB)
    grey edge
              colorcorrect.algorithm.grey_edge
              usage: image,order(default:1),p(default:6),sigma(default:2.0),saturation_threshold(default:255)
              illuminant from the p-norm of Gaussian derivatives of order 1 or 2;
              order=0 is the general grey world (Shades of Gray with smoothing).
              estimate_illuminant_grey_edge returns the estimate itself.
    pca color constancy
              colorcorrect.algorithm.pca_color_constancy
              usage: image,fraction(default:0.035),saturation_threshold(default:255)
              Cheng et al. 2014: PCA of the brightest/darkest pixels along the mean colour.
              estimate_illuminant_pca returns the estimate itself.
    multi scale retinex (MSR / MSRCR)
              colorcorrect.algorithm.multi_scale_retinex
              usage: image,sigmas(default:(15,80,250)),color_restoration(default:True),
                     alpha(default:125),beta(default:46),clip(default:0.01)
              Jobson et al. 1997 with the simplest colour balance output scaling of
              the IPOL implementation (Petro, Sbert, Morel 2014).
    random spray retinex
              colorcorrect.algorithm.random_spray_retinex
              usage: image,sprays(default:10),points(default:250),radius(default:None),
                     seed(default:None),workers(default:None)
              Provenzi et al. 2007; cost grows with sprays*points*pixels (C++, threaded).

    RACE / sigma-RACE
              colorcorrect.algorithm.race
              usage: image,sprays(default:10),points(default:250),alpha(default:20),
                     regularize(default:True),radius(default:None),seed(default:None),workers(default:None)
              Provenzi et al. 2008: spray fusion of RSR (local white patch) and ACE
              (local grey world); regularize=True is sigma-RACE, which keeps uniform
              areas by mixing with the original according to local/global contrast.
    gray pixel / grayness index
              colorcorrect.algorithm.gray_pixel
              usage: image,fraction(default:0.001),epsilon(default:1e-4),saturation_threshold(default:255)
              Qian et al. 2019: illuminant = mean of the 0.1% pixels with the smallest
              grayness index (grayness_index returns the map,
              estimate_illuminant_grayness_index the estimate).
    weighted grey edge
              colorcorrect.algorithm.weighted_grey_edge
              usage: image,weighting(default:"specular"),kappa(default:10),p(default:1),sigma(default:2.0),
                     iterations(default:10),tolerance(default:0.1),saturation_threshold(default:255)
              Gijsenij et al. 2012: grey edge with photometric edge weights
              (specular / shadow / material), applied iteratively.
              estimate_illuminant_weighted_grey_edge returns the estimate itself.

All functions take and return (height, width, 3) uint8 numpy arrays and never
modify their input.  The illuminant estimation methods (grey world, shades of
gray, grey edge, weighted grey edge, PCA, gray pixel) assume a diagonal
(von Kries) colour cast and keep G as the reference channel.

sample usage::

    >>> from PIL import Image
    >>> import colorcorrect.algorithm as cca
    >>> from colorcorrect.util import from_pil, to_pil
    >>> img = Image.open('/path/to/image')
    >>> to_pil(cca.stretch(from_pil(img))).show()

